  "final_answer": "The RSI 30/70 strategy lost 14.7% on MSFT over the past year. Just holding the stock would have made about 1.1% over the same period.\n\n**Settings:** RSI 14-period (the default), buying below 30 and selling above 70. It ran on daily bars from 2025-09-29 to 2026-09-25 (the default one-year window), starting with $10,000.\n\n**Results**\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Total return | -14.66% ($10,000 to $8,533.55) |\n| Buy and hold, same window | +1.13% (close $510.40 to $516.17) |\n| Max drawdown | -29.5% |\n| Sharpe / Sortino / Calmar | -0.77 / -1.00 / -0.70 |\n| Trades | 2 (1 win, 1 loss) |\n| Profit factor | 0.23 |\n| Average holding time | 59.5 days |\n| Tool's grade | F |\n\n**Trades**\n1. Bought 2025-11-17 at $503.85 and sold 2026-04-15 at $409.15, a loss of 19.0% (-$1,895.77). RSI never got back above 70 while MSFT kept falling, so the strategy held the whole slide. There is no stop-loss, so nothing cut the loss short.\n2. Bought 2026-06-18 at $379.07 and sold 2026-07-16 at $399.95, a gain of 5.3% (+$429.32).\n\nAfter mid-July the strategy was in cash for the rest of the window, so it missed MSFT's move from about $400 back to $516.\n\n**Caveats**\n- Two trades is far too few to judge the strategy. This result mostly shows how this one year went for MSFT, not whether the rule works.\n- The tool reports an annual return of -20.7%, which doesn't match a -14.7% total over about one year. It looks like a different annualizing method. Use the total return.\n- The tool's automatic comments are generic. It lists \"Consistent performance\" as a strength, which two trades can't show, and calls a 29.5% drawdown \"Medium\" risk.\n\n**Possible next steps:** run a longer window (for example 5+ years) to get more trades, try other thresholds such as 35/65 to trade more often, or compare RSI against other strategies on MSFT.",
